# Galactic Map

  While society has spread across the galaxy, it is largely constrained to the Orion-Perseus arms. Civilization is not particularly dense, having been spread out during the Great Confederate Expansion, and has stagnated with the limitated availability of FTL technology.

    <GuideEntityEmbed Entity="GalaxyMap" Caption="galactic population map"/>

### Population Density
While many galactic map charts from various cartographer organizations seem to depict the entire two arms as being densely populated, in reality almost 99% of the "occupied" territory is empty. This is due to the limited viable prospects for colonization and habitation in a large majority of probed systems, while the few systems with value are often difficult in their own ways to settle.
A vast majority of population in the galaxy is centralized to specific, habitable and/or economically viable systems.

### The Crab Nebula
After humanity first stepped foot outside of the Solar System and into deepspace, a common standard for direction became critical - a lack of a consistent point of view caused chaos, seen in the early days when the Crab standard was not yet widespread.
The Crab nebula was the choice reference point, being located parallel to both Earth and to the center of the galaxy. Even today, most shuttle piloting software still uses Crab North.

### Remains of the Solar System
It has been a long time since humanity has left the solar system in pursuit of greater fortunes. However, the cost of the departure remains, scarring the whole system; Most viable sources of material have been mined out, Earth's climate has collapsed, and many of the system's
original residents have since left to pursue the new border of progress past the belts.

### Logistics
Despite some advanced by corporations of old, viable interstellar significantly-faster-than-light travel has largely remained a dream. While many developed technologies do allow for technical faster-than-light travel, these options are limited for manned transport. Jump drives utilizing reality distortion do exist, but
are not capable of prolonged travel, with limited reliable travel distance; The farthest jump a single system has achieved was only 50 kilometers, and even then the circumstances were far beyond what could be consistently and safely replicated.
Exceptionally long distance communication is also possible through quantum encoding advances allowing for near-instantaneus communication, however it is limited by low bandwidth without extremely developed infrastructure.
Despite these logistical limitations, some progress has been made with travel speed, with advanced cruising systems and cryosleep nonetheless allowing for long distance travel at technically faster-than-light speeds. Cruising FTL, while capable of going up to 100 times as fast as conventional travel,
is still slow due to the astronomical distances separating planetary systems, and requires dedicated vessels for safe operation.
This travel time limitation has been the bane of many a space government, forcing the need for couriers for transportation of significant data, and creating long delays for fleet movements.
